    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>Aluminium fencing (aluminum fencing, if you are reading in the US) is widely regarded as one of the most durable, low-maintenance fence options for homes and commercial properties alike. Unlike timber, which can rot and warp, or wrought iron, which is prone to rust, aluminium usually has a powder-coated finish that stands up well to the weather. However, "low maintenance" does not mean "no maintenance". A simple seasonal routine helps the fence keep its strength and looks for as long as possible.</p>
<p>By working through the year season by season, you can catch minor issues before they escalate, protect the coating and keep your garden looking its best. This guide breaks down what to do every spring, summer, autumn and winter. Always read your fence manufacturer's care guide too, as finishes and hardware differ.</p>

<h2 id="spring-cleaning">1. Spring cleaning</h2>
<p>As the dampness of winter fades, spring is the most important time for a deep clean. Over the winter your fence will have picked up grime, road salt spray and organic residue. Spring cleaning is not just about looks: it removes corrosive material that could eventually damage the powder coating.</p>
<p>Start by rinsing the whole fence with a garden hose. Avoid blasting it with a pressure washer: if the coating already has small chips, high pressure can lift it further. If your manufacturer allows pressure washing, follow their guidance on pressure and nozzle distance, and use a low setting and a fan nozzle.</p>
<p>Wash with warm water and a little mild washing-up liquid. For stubborn spots such as bird droppings or dried mud, use a soft-bristle brush or a non-abrasive sponge, never a scouring pad or wire brush. Pay close attention to the bottom rails, where dirt splashes up from the ground. Rinse thoroughly afterwards so no soapy film remains, as soap that dries in the sun can leave streaks.</p>

<h2 id="structural-inspection">2. Structural inspection</h2>
<p>As the ground thaws, the soil often shifts. Frost heave can push fence posts out of line, so spring is the ideal time to walk the boundary and give each post a firm "shake test".</p>
<p>Each post should feel solid in its footing. If any post moves, check the footing: look for soil that has washed away or sunk, cracked concrete or a post that has started to lean. Topping up and firming soil around a post set in earth can help, but a loose post set in concrete usually needs resetting, which is a job for a fencer if you are not confident doing it yourself.</p>
<p>At the same time, inspect the hardware. Check the screws and brackets that connect the panels to the posts. Many aluminium panels are designed to "rack" to follow a slope, but the fixings still need to stay tight for the fence to be stable. If you find rusty fasteners, which can happen when plain steel screws were used, replace them promptly with stainless steel fixings or the ones supplied by the manufacturer, before rust streaks stain the panels.</p>

<h2 id="vegetation-control">3. Summer: vegetation control</h2>
<p>As the weather warms up, plant growth speeds up. A vine-covered fence might look romantic, but heavy growth can work against aluminium fencing. Thick vines trap moisture against the metal, and as they grow their weight can put strain on the rails.</p>
<p>Through the summer, keep a buffer of about 15 cm (6 in) between the fence and any heavy shrubs or climbers. If you use a strimmer (string trimmer) along the fence line, be careful: the fast-spinning line can nick the powder coating at the base of the posts. Those small nicks expose the bare aluminium underneath. Aluminium does not rust like iron, but exposed metal can oxidise, and over time the coating around a chip may bubble or peel. A manual edger, or a stone or mulch strip along the base of the fence, saves you trimming right up against it.</p>

<h2 id="hardware-lubrication">4. Summer: gates and hardware</h2>
<p>Summer is usually the busiest season for gates, with children, pets and guests coming and going. Hinges and latches take the most wear. A squeak or resistance when you open the gate is a sign of friction that can lead to the hardware failing.</p>
<p>Lubricate all moving parts once each summer with a dry silicone-based or PTFE lubricant, or whatever the gate hardware maker recommends. Avoid heavy oils, and remember that general-purpose oil sprays can attract grit, which builds up into a grinding paste that wears the parts. A dry lubricant stays cleaner and keeps the action smooth.</p>
<p>While you are at the gate, check the drop rod (the long bolt that holds one leaf of a double gate in place). Make sure the hole in the ground or driveway is clear of debris so the rod drops in fully and the gate cannot swing and catch the wind.</p>
<p>If the fence is a pool barrier, the gate should self-close and self-latch, and the latch must work every time. Check the pool-barrier rules where you live (US codes commonly require this; in the UK, follow the manufacturer's and safety guidance), and test the gate weekly during the swimming season. If it does not close and latch on its own from any open position, fix it before the pool is used again.</p>

<h2 id="debris-removal">5. Autumn: debris removal</h2>
<p>When autumn arrives, the main threat to your fence is decomposing organic matter. Falling leaves collect in the corners and pile up against the bottom rail, and wet leaves hold moisture against the finish for long periods.</p>
<p>Make it a habit to blow or rake leaves away from the fence line as part of your regular garden tidy-up. Piles of wet leaves left against the aluminium into winter can stain the finish. Autumn is also the time to look for pitting: if you live near the coast or a busy road, salt and grime can cause tiny pits in the finish. One last clean in late autumn removes these contaminants before winter snow and ice settle on them.</p>

<h2 id="surface-restoration">6. Autumn: surface touch-ups</h2>
<p>Before the first frosts, give the fence a careful look for scratches and chips in the powder coating. These usually come from garden machinery, falling branches or gravel thrown up by cars. Many marks are purely cosmetic, but they can grow if left alone.</p>
<p>Use a touch-up kit matched to your fence by the manufacturer, which is usually available for their standard colours. Apply a thin layer with a fine brush to any bare metal, on a dry day and within the temperature range given on the product label, so the paint cures properly. This small job seals the chip, keeps the fence looking seamless and stops dirt and moisture creeping under the edges of the original coating.</p>

<h2 id="snow-management">7. Winter: snow management</h2>
<p>Winter is the hardest season for any outdoor structure. Aluminium is strong, but it is also fairly flexible, and the weight of deep snow drifted or piled against the panels can bend pickets out of shape.</p>
<p>When you clear the drive or path, do not pile snow against the fence. If a heavy fall leaves a deep drift along the fence line, gently shovel it away. Take care not to strike the fence with a metal shovel, as a hard knock can chip the coating, and cold weather makes chips more likely.</p>

<h2 id="de-icers">8. Winter: salt and de-icers</h2>
<p>The biggest winter threat to an aluminium fence is often not the cold but the de-icing salt used on roads and paths. Rock salt is corrosive to metal, and a fence next to a pavement or gritted road can end up coated in salt spray for much of the winter.</p>
<p>Whenever there is a mild spell and temperatures rise above freezing, rinse the fence with clean water to wash off salt residue, so it does not sit on the finish for months. On your own property, keep rock salt away from the fence and use a de-icer that your fence or surface manufacturer approves, applied sparingly. Once the cold weather is over, give the fence and its base a thorough rinse.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Do the shake test and gate check at the start of every season, not just in spring. It only takes a few minutes, and a post or latch that gets slightly worse each time is much easier to fix early.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="summary">Seasonal checklist at a glance</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spring:</strong> deep clean and tighten: wash off winter salt and grime, shake-test the posts, check the fixings.</li>
<li><strong>Summer:</strong> trim and lubricate: keep plants back from the fence, lubricate hinges and latches, check the drop rod and any pool gate.</li>
<li><strong>Autumn:</strong> clear and touch up: remove wet leaves, clean once more, seal chips in the coating.</li>
<li><strong>Winter:</strong> snow and salt control: keep drifts off the panels, rinse off de-icing salt in mild spells.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Can I use a pressure washer on my aluminium fence?</h3>
<p>Usually it is not needed, and it is best avoided unless the manufacturer allows it. High pressure can lift the powder coating around any existing nicks. A garden hose with a spray nozzle and a soft brush is safer and usually enough. If you do use one, follow the manufacturer's guidance and use a low setting and a fan nozzle, held well back.</p>
<h3>Why is my gate sticking even though I lubricated it?</h3>
<p>If lubrication does not help, the problem is likely alignment. Check whether the ground has shifted, a post has leaned or the hinges have slipped. You may need to adjust the hinges or have the gate post reset so the latch lines up properly.</p>
<h3>Does aluminium rust like iron?</h3>
<p>No, aluminium does not form red rust. It can, however, corrode or oxidise, which usually shows as a white, chalky powder, and this mainly happens where the protective coating is damaged. Regular cleaning and prompt touch-ups help prevent it.</p>
<h3>How do I remove hard water marks from my fence?</h3>
<p>White spots from sprinklers are usually mineral deposits. Wipe them with a diluted mild cleaner suitable for powder-coated metal, tested on a hidden spot first, then rinse with fresh water.</p>
<h3>Is it okay to let ivy grow on an aluminium fence?</h3>
<p>It is not recommended. Ivy will not rot the metal the way it can damage timber, but it adds weight, traps moisture that can lead to the coating peeling, and makes it hard to inspect the fence for problems.</p>
<h3>How long will an aluminium fence last?</h3>
<p>It depends on the quality of the fence and its coating, how well it was installed, your climate (coastal and salted-road locations are harder on it) and how well it is maintained. With regular care a good aluminium fence can last a long time; check the manufacturer's warranty for the term it states.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A little care each season keeps an aluminium fence straight, secure and smart, and stops a small chip or loose screw from turning into a bigger repair. If you are planning a new boundary, read our guide to <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-mistakes">privacy fence mistakes</a> and what drives <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-cost">privacy fence cost</a>, and if you have aluminium railings on a deck, see <a href="/blog/deck-railing-maintenance-by-season">deck railing maintenance by season</a>. Explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service for the garden spaces your fence frames, and to plan your outdoor space with a designer, <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
